# Atomic Changes and Commit Batching

## 1) Prefer atomic, phased updates

- Avoid very large codebase changes that touch many files at once unless explicitly requested.
- Break implementation into clear phases/sequences/batches that can be reviewed and committed independently.
- Each phase should focus on one coherent outcome (for example: schema setup, API routes, UI integration, tests, docs).

## 2) Control scope before editing

- Before substantial edits, define the immediate phase and keep file changes limited to that phase.
- Prefer completing one phase fully (tests-first implementation + quick validation) before moving to the next.
- If a task grows beyond reasonable scope, split it into the next batch instead of expanding the current one.
- For test-driven phases, keep active test scope limited to the current phase's expected outcomes.

## 3) Commit guidance per completed phase

- After each completed phase/batch, provide a suggested commit message.
- If multiple repositories are modified in the same phase, provide a separate commit message suggestion for each repository.
- Commit messages should describe intent and outcome clearly (what changed and why), and remain concise.

## 4) Communicate batching clearly

- In status updates and final summaries, explicitly label completed work by phase/batch.
- Include what was done now vs what is intentionally deferred to the next phase.
